## Reference

https://github.com/compio-rs/cyper/issues/25

This was first discovered when I saw CPU usage spikes in a server running `cyper` in `compio` runtime, which uses io_uring under the hood. Normally in `compio` IO requests submitted do not finish immediately, which is causing issues here.

I am writing a custom protocol relying on HTTP/2. The protocol relies on maintaing integrity of data frames between sender and receiver, meaning that I need to avoid a situation where a data frame gets involuntarily split by the application layer (h2 library). I noticed that the implementation in hyper is not satisfactory, as it relies on h2 internal flow control buffering which will send a chunk of a submitted frame as soon as there is some increment in capacity:
https://github.com/hyperium/hyper/blob/eaf2267cdc148604469fb09da22646f31710107a/src/proto/h2/mod.rs#L121-L124

I was thinking of using `poll_capacity` in h2 to specificially wait until there is capacity for the entire data frame to fit, prior to calling `send_data`. I am aware that `poll_capacity` may need to be called multiple times, until the full amount of capacity is assigned. In my prototyping, this works very well:

```
/// Should only be called after a non-0 capacity was requested for the stream.
pub fn wait_for_capacity(stream: h2::SendStream<Bytes>, target: usize) -> WaitForCapacity {
    WaitForCapacity {
        stream: Some(stream),
        requested_capacity: target,
    }
}

pub struct WaitForCapacity {
    stream: Option<h2::SendStream<Bytes>>,
    requested_capacity: usize,
}

impl WaitForCapacity {
    fn stream(&mut self) -> &mut h2::SendStream<Bytes> {
        self.stream.as_mut().unwrap()
    }
}

impl Future for WaitForCapacity {
    type Output = h2::SendStream<Bytes>;

    fn poll(mut self: Pin<&mut Self>, cx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<Self::Output> {
        loop {
            let increment = ready!(self.stream().poll_capacity(cx)).unwrap();

            let act = self.stream().capacity();
