the following code copies in stream to out. it does not send any frames out before the incomming stream isnt closed. not even the headers are sent out before that.
i.e. neither send_data nor send_response  do anything. When the incomming stream is finally closed, header and data are sent in one package, which makes me believe they have been buffered internally to wait for instream closure intentionally. Thety're also send squashed in one DATA frame instead of multiple, completely defeating the purpose of the DATA frame.

"H2 recv frame" is seen on stdout only _once_ even when the client keeps sending (confirmed with wireshark).

```rust
fn handle_request(
    identity: String,
    req: http::Request<h2::RecvStream>,
    mut resp: h2::server::SendResponse<bytes::Bytes>,
) -> impl Future<Item=(), Error=h2::Error> {
    let response = Response::builder().status(StatusCode::OK).body(()).unwrap();

    resp.send_response(response, false)
        .into_future()
        .and_then(|mut send| {
            req.into_body()
                .fold(send, |mut send, frame| {
                    info!("H2 recv frame {:?}", frame);
                    if let Err(e) = send.send_data(frame, false) {
                        error!(" -> err={:?}", e);
                    }
                    future::ok::<_, h2::Error>(send)
                })
                .and_then(|mut send| {
                    info!("H2 closing");
                    // close send stream when recv closed
                    if let Err(e) = send.send_data(Bytes::new(), true) {
                        error!(" -> err={:?}", e);
                    }
                    Ok(())
                })
        })
}

